      "inputTokens": 176,
      "outputTokens": 1260,
      "costUsd": 0.011427999999999999,
      "pronunciationText": "A sequence like eighty, eighty-six, ninety-two is called an arithmetic sequence, or an arithmetic progression, AP for short. It's arithmetic when every difference between a term and the term before it is the same. That fixed difference is called the common difference, d. Here, eighty-six minus eighty is six, ninety-two minus eighty-six is six, and so on, so d is six. The difference can be negative, too. Twenty, seventeen, fourteen, eleven goes down by three each step, so d is minus three. But one, four, nine, sixteen isn't arithmetic. Its differences are three, five and seven, which aren't the same. So to test a list of terms, check that each difference matches. For a sequence given by a rule, show the difference is the same for every n."
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-03",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-f02/scene-03.wav",
