      "inputTokens": 154,
      "outputTokens": 1044,
      "costUsd": 0.009473,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's why arithmetic sequences look so familiar. Plot each stack's height against the number of chairs. Every extra chair goes up by d, the same amount each time, so the points lie on a straight line. Expand the formula, and you get d n, plus a minus d. That's a linear function of n, and its gradient is d. For the chairs, a sub n is six n plus seventy-four, so the line has gradient six. It would cross the vertical axis at seventy-four, but there's no stack of zero chairs, because n starts at one. And the points sit only at whole numbers, because you can't stack part of a chair, so we don't join them up."
    },
