      "costUsd": 0.0078035000000000005,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's a useful twist. Can a stack be exactly one hundred and fifty centimetres tall? Set the formula equal to one hundred and fifty. Eighty, plus n minus one lots of six, equals one hundred and fifty. So n minus one is seventy over six, and n is twelve and two thirds. That's not a whole number, and you can't have two thirds of a chair. So one hundred and fifty is not a term. On the graph, the line passes one hundred and fifty between the twelfth and thirteenth points, which are one hundred and forty-six and one hundred and fifty-two."
    },
